WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.557
Film Courage: Shannon was it your&nbsp;
goal to become a studio executive?

00:00:02.557 --> 00:00:03.480
Writer/Instructor and Script Consultant Shannan E.&nbsp;
Johnson (The Professional Pen): In the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:08.760
of my career I was a development and current&nbsp;
programming executive on the television side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.680
Once I left and I went off to do Broadway national&nbsp;
tours and I was doing dance companies and all this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.520
other stuff that has absolutely nothing to do&nbsp;
with writing, when I came back to Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.040
it was my intention to get back into development&nbsp;
whether that was at a network or a studio or at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.620
smaller production company. I was pretty open but&nbsp;
I realized that I liked to work in the area where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.600
you take people from idea to production and so I&nbsp;
probably would have stayed in development but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.000
would have necessarily been at a network or studio&nbsp;
or a production company just wherever I felt like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.980
I would be able to come in and actually be seen&nbsp;
and used as a person with all of the knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.880
that I have to offer which can be hard when you&nbsp;
go on any job you want to make sure that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.540
going to be able to come and be able to bring&nbsp;
something to the table so I was looking forward to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:56.580
being able to do that and obviously it didn't go&nbsp;
that way that's how I ended up back into the kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:03.060
of scrips consultant area how did you become a&nbsp;
studio executive so back in the day there used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:08.400
be a program called the entertainment Associates&nbsp;
program at NBC Universal so a lot of people know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.920
about Fellowship programs for screenwriters but&nbsp;
they're also used to be programs for people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:19.020
are interested in being executives now there are a&nbsp;
few more that are coming up Lena Waithe's company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.340
Hillman Grad has one diverse representation&nbsp;
has one but back then the entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.440
Associates program at NBC Universal was one of&nbsp;
the larger ones for television and so there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.300
an application process and I don't know how many&nbsp;
people they would choose each cycle but my cycle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:40.140
they chose five people I happen to be the only&nbsp;
one who was coming from outside of California&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:46.740
because I was in graduate school when I applied&nbsp;
I was in film school at the time so of the five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:52.080
people that were chosen two of us weren't already&nbsp;
working at NBC Universal and I was the only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:58.680
who came from out of state but that program&nbsp;
was basically to help you to jump from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:03.720
assistant desk into an executive role because&nbsp;
it's very hard to make that transition simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:09.180
by sheer numbers there aren't as many executive&nbsp;
positions as there are assistant positions and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.840
if you can imagine everyone on the same floor&nbsp;
let's say there are three executives but five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.760
assistants not everybody's going to be able to&nbsp;
get that one executive position when it opens up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.800
so this program was to help you to get the skills&nbsp;
that were necessary and help you to kind of jump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.440
past that assistant desk and become an executive&nbsp;
and so I was able to come in and be a an executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.420
at the Sci-Fi channel and basically learn the&nbsp;
whole business and learn the whole craft while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:39.420
I was there we always see it in our comments you&nbsp;
know Hollywood is all based on nepotism who you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:45.060
know did you know anyone to get into the program&nbsp;
no I didn't and I think that was a good thing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:50.520
a bad thing because it proves that not everyone's&nbsp;
path is the same so again most of the people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:55.020
got in the program were already people who worked&nbsp;
at NBC Universal so that means they already had an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.940
upper hand on all of the other applicants in the&nbsp;
program because I was in in film school at Florida&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.600
State University which means I was in the State of&nbsp;
Florida I didn't know anything about this program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.220
I did not know how coveted it was I didn't even&nbsp;
know what development meant I can be honest about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:12.720
that something about film school is that most&nbsp;
film schools teach you the creative side they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.760
don't teach you the business side so even though&nbsp;
I was in film school for two whole years I didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:23.040
come out understanding what development was or&nbsp;
understanding what current programming meant or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.780
anything about the business side of it let alone&nbsp;
the TV business side of it because again most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.460
film school programs are going to teach you about&nbsp;
features because they're really building writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:38.880
writer directors more than they're building all&nbsp;
of the other pieces of the puzzle so so yes it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.700
true that it's mostly about who you know in this&nbsp;
particular situation it was about my skill that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.440
all it could be because I didn't know you know I&nbsp;
didn't know anybody I had interned before so I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.480
already lived in Los Angeles before I had already&nbsp;
interned at a lot of places I had already been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.320
on several sets because I wanted to kind of learn&nbsp;
the ropes of how to do everything in the business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:04.860
but I think I must have had an awesome application&nbsp;
and my application other than me being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:08.100
talk about myself I don't really remember much&nbsp;
about the application but we had to do coverage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:13.680
I didn't even know what coverage was but I had a&nbsp;
natural knack for storytelling and obviously I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.700
been in a screenwriting program for two years and&nbsp;
all we did was sit around and get notes to each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:23.160
other and as I've told you guys before it was&nbsp;
there that one of my instructors said well why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:29.160
are you so good at helping other people with their&nbsp;
stuff and so that's what made me seek information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:35.940
on are there people out there who just help other&nbsp;
people write and I think that's how I got to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:41.400
word development which I think is how it got&nbsp;
me to the entertainment associates program and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:48.060
that's how I was able to apply and so the program&nbsp;
so it had the the rounds of applications we had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:53.880
do coverage and then we had maybe we had a phone&nbsp;
interview I don't remember but then they also flew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.300
me in I was the only person who's coming from out&nbsp;
so I don't know if other people who got to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:02.760
point also got flown in but we got flown in we had&nbsp;
to meet with several different departments so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:08.040
met with people in diversity I met with people in&nbsp;
TV I met with people in in film I met with people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:15.780
all over the lot and just me being me I just&nbsp;
remember being very honest when they would ask me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:19.560
questions about well what are you watching and why&nbsp;
and why do you like this and why don't you like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:24.780
that and so I remember sitting in one particular&nbsp;
office and I'm at NBC Universal and they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:29.640
asking me about the things that I was watching and&nbsp;
liking and two of those things happened to be NBC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:35.580
shows and they wanted to know well why are you&nbsp;
saying these things about the shows and I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:40.680
know if other people would have been backed up and&nbsp;
tried to find something positive to say you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:45.540
what I mean but for me it was like well here are&nbsp;
the reasons and so one of them was Friday Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:52.800
Lights which I think is one of the best TV shows&nbsp;
to ever be made however as a Texas girl who grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.720
up with Family Guy, football even though I could&nbsp;
care less about football what I understood was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:03.060
you have Friday night it's coming on at a Friday&nbsp;
night guess where everybody in Texas is at their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:07.800
own football games so it seems to me that you've&nbsp;
set this show up for failure that's basically you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:13.140
know what I said to them and then another show&nbsp;
that that was out on at the time was Heroes I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:17.460
really loved Heroes I actually did a speck of&nbsp;
Heroes and that's what got me into the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:22.920
round of the Warner Brothers writing fellowship&nbsp;
at that time and I said you know season one was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:27.780
awesome but in season two you broke all of the&nbsp;
rules and therefore now we're a little bit lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:33.360
about what this show really is and I said that to&nbsp;
them not even considering myself you know giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:39.240
a critique or giving notes or anything like that&nbsp;
that's just how I felt about it naturally and I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.540
just going to go say maybe that's the thing that&nbsp;
got me in the program you know what I mean because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:47.100
they could see that I could explain to them what&nbsp;
the issue was and how I felt like you could fix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:53.880
that issue so even though nepotism in the way of&nbsp;
who you know not necessarily who you're related to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:58.860
may have gotten some of the people in the program&nbsp;
I know for me it was pure applique education&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:03.900
thousands of people applied and I happened to&nbsp;
be picked as one of the five people to do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:10.020
what was your day-to-day job as a creative&nbsp;
executive so here's the full transparency of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:17.460
it I did not have any idea so I walked into this&nbsp;
program thinking it was going to be a program that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:22.260
taught me how to be a creative executive what&nbsp;
I didn't understand already and it's probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:27.480
because I was coming from the outside in which is&nbsp;
why I said earlier about the the good and the bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:33.600
of it so the good of it is it's possible you can&nbsp;
come from the outside and come in the bad of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:38.220
is I don't have any information because I've been&nbsp;
on the outside so I don't know what a development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:43.440
executive does I applied for this because I&nbsp;
thought it would be a program and I like to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:50.100
in a classroom setting but that's not what this&nbsp;
was it was a job so here I am sitting at my desk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:57.000
what am I supposed to be doing the other people&nbsp;
who were already in the industry who are already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.540
assistants and coordinators knew what they&nbsp;
should jump in to do because they had already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:05.820
been covering the desk of executives they had&nbsp;
already been seeing and being been exposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:11.760
what executives did I had no exposure I had no&nbsp;
nothing I thought I'm going to get a desk and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:16.440
people are going to give me screenplays and I'm&nbsp;
going to tell them what's good bad what needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:23.160
be worked on that's what I thought the job was it&nbsp;
was not the job was it part of it sure but a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:30.300
very tiny part of it so I ended up having a slower&nbsp;
learning curve than the rest of the people in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:36.960
cohort they were able to get in and go meanwhile&nbsp;
I sat around a lot waiting on someone to tell me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:43.380
what am I supposed to be doing I didn't even know&nbsp;
what questions to be asking and that can be very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:47.880
discouraging to a person and I'm someone who's&nbsp;
always on top of it I'm very confident I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:52.620
what I'm doing Etc so it was very discouraging to&nbsp;
me because I felt like failure or I felt like I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:57.000
had been set up to fail even though I'm sure&nbsp;
that's not that wasn't anyone's intention so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:03.840
once I finally realized what I should be doing my&nbsp;
day-to-day could be taking meetings with people as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:09.060
an executive it's your job to kind of be a mental&nbsp;
Rolodex of Hollywood you should know the managers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:12.180
you should know the agents you should know the&nbsp;
directors you should know the writers you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:16.860
know the up and coming writers because if you're&nbsp;
in a room and someone says hey we need it's your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:23.400
job to go I know a few people let me contact them&nbsp;
let me get their their samples let me figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:28.980
how to pull them into the conversation so a lot&nbsp;
of that was that and so since I was new to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:34.620
game and had come from the outside I needed to&nbsp;
start my my Rolodex so I had to figure out how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:40.440
reach out to people and be able to contact them&nbsp;
just so that we can start a relationship and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:45.420
that's why people may hear general meeting often&nbsp;
because some of that that basically just means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:51.060
let's just get on the phone or let's just have a a&nbsp;
an in-person visit so that we can just get to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:54.840
each other so I can know what kind of writer you&nbsp;
are you can know a little bit about me I can add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:59.160
you to my mental Rolodex it doesn't mean you came&nbsp;
in for a job just means you came in so that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:02.580
have a conversation and kind of get to know each&nbsp;
other it's like a date all it doesn't mean we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:05.700
going to be boyfriend and girlfriend at the end&nbsp;
but it does mean that we got to know each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:11.340
a little better so I could be doing that I could&nbsp;
be taking pitches so that means people who think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:16.260
that our brand is for their project would come in&nbsp;
and Pitch it to us and we'd be able to listen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:21.720
it ask questions whatever it is we have and then&nbsp;
it would be our job as the more Junior executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:27.300
to go in and then pitch to our EVP about what we&nbsp;
just saw and if we think that it's something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:33.540
we should have here at our on our Network and at&nbsp;
the network that I was at which is sci-fi we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:38.640
unscripted and scripted and we had development&nbsp;
and current all happening in one place and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:44.040
for everyone who doesn't know the difference so&nbsp;
scripted is going to be your TV shows like friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:48.540
where it's actually scripted you have writers who&nbsp;
have been brought on a writing staff to write the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:53.640
screenplays whereas unscripted is what we kind of&nbsp;
know as reality TV some of it can be docu-series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:58.560
some of it can be a little dramatic so it might&nbsp;
have a tiny piece of scripted to it but it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:04.080
mean that they're writing screenplays and then&nbsp;
development means we are developing the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:09.120
to be on air so this is this is the person who if&nbsp;
you're a new screenwriter or even if you're not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:12.780
new screenwriter but you have a project and you&nbsp;
want someone to buy it you're going to pitch it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:17.760
to the development people those are the people who&nbsp;
are going to help you to get it to the stage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:22.440
production then your current programming people&nbsp;
are the people who then take the baby after you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:26.340
get it to production and take it on for the rest&nbsp;
of its life so however long it's going to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:32.280
on television it stays in the current programming&nbsp;
department so that would mean that features don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:37.560
have a current programming department because&nbsp;
features are not on television so since I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:41.880
in TV we were doing all of the above and in&nbsp;
my position I got to kind of move around and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:46.380
do all of the above so whether we were taking&nbsp;
pitches for a scripted or taking pictures for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:54.300
unscripted or I was looking at the scripts coming&nbsp;
in from the writing staffs for the current shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.300 --> 00:12:00.960
so at that time let's say it was Stargate Universe&nbsp;
so every single script that was going to go on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:05.760
air had to come to the executives first for&nbsp;
our round of notes before it was going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:10.020
approved to be able to go into production then&nbsp;
once it got into production they would send us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:14.280
dailies which is basically what they've recorded&nbsp;
the whole day what they've shot that whole day I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:20.760
would also go through those dailies then they&nbsp;
would send us the the drafts of of each of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:26.340
episodes and so now I'm going through and I am&nbsp;
kind of like an editor but obviously not doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:31.440
the editing because there's a post-production team&nbsp;
but now I'm going through and I'm mark talking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:37.980
you know at five minutes in you know here's a note&nbsp;
at seven minutes in here's a note etc so that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:43.020
can kind of go back to make sure that it's going&nbsp;
to fit what the executives are wanting it to fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:50.400
we would also this was a shocker for me we&nbsp;
were also in charge of marketing and I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:54.540
thinking I don't have a marketing degree you know&nbsp;
that's not my job so I'm thinking the marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:58.620
department is going to come in here and pitch&nbsp;
to us what they think we should do with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:03.060
show and we're going to go go yes that's it&nbsp;
or no that's not it that wasn't the case we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:08.040
as the executives who were more intimate with&nbsp;
the project would pitch to the marketing people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:12.480
and then the marketing people would take&nbsp;
it and execute it that was new for me so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:16.200
that was a learning curve it was helpful though&nbsp;
because now I know how to market my own business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:23.580
same thing with branding casting wardrobe&nbsp;
everything and executive literally does every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:30.900
piece of the public puzzle or has their hand in&nbsp;
every part of the process and so even though I I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:36.000
got into it for the script part which again I&nbsp;
did I had to read a lot of screenplays just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:41.160
you know when people submit their screenplays and&nbsp;
do a little coverage to be able to say okay this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:45.900
is some this is a writer that maybe we can work&nbsp;
with later or this is an idea that maybe we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:50.940
work with them to actually develop or we're not&nbsp;
going to do either the above it's not for us or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:55.860
the writer is just not at the level that we want&nbsp;
to work with now so I did a lot of that sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.860 --> 00:14:00.240
I had to read just to read so that I can get to&nbsp;
know what writers were out there what new writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:06.660
were coming so sure reading and giving notes was&nbsp;
a part of the process but because I worked in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:12.060
development and current a lot of it was some of&nbsp;
that stuff that you would never even consider a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:18.060
an executive has their hand in and so it was a big&nbsp;
learning curve for me so what I enjoyed about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:23.340
